                "text": "**Analyzing Grid Transformation**\n\nOkay, I'm focusing on the grid transformations. It's clear that the 3x3 input grids map onto 12x12 output grids. Breaking the 12x12 down, it's actually 4x4 blocks, where each block is a 3x3 grid, allowing for a structured analysis. I'm exploring the mapping of each 3x3 block B[r, c] within the output. The aim is to thoroughly determine how the input gets expanded into those blocks.\n\n\n**Confirming Transformation Pattern**\n\nThe initial suspicion of a simple rotation has been dismissed; a transpose operation now appears correct. Specifically, the input grid is transposed to generate each 3x3 block within the 12x12 output. I'm confirming this by expanding the analysis to include the other B[r,c] components.\n\n\n**Investigating Replication Patterns**\n\nI've made a breakthrough! My focus has shifted from simple transposition and rotation towards replication. It's now apparent that each 3x3 block B[r,c] in the output grid repeats vertically and horizontally. This repetition forms larger 6x12 blocks, which is where things get interesting. I'm digging into how the source is replicated across the B[r,c] blocks.\n\n\n**Defining Replication Strategy**\n\nI've realized the output grid is a 2x2 meta-grid of 6x6 blocks, each formed by tiling a 3x3 block 2x2 times. Checking this in Example 2, each 6x6 quadrant is just the 3x3 pattern repeated 2x2. Now, my focus is on precisely defining these four 3x3 patterns (TL, TR, BL, BR) within each quadrant, and how they relate to the input. Specifically, I'm examining Example 2 to understand the input's mapping to those quadrants.\n\n\n**Confirming 180\u00b0 Rotation**\n\nI've got it! The TL block is the input grid rotated 180 degrees. I tested this against Example 1 and it's spot on. The transformation is confirmed by mapping the original coordinates using (r, c) -> (2-r, 2-c), which validates that, indeed, it's a 180-degree rotation. Now, I'm examining how the TR block relates to the input.\n\n\n**Determining TR Block Mapping**\n\nOkay, I've got a grasp on how the top right (TR) block gets generated. After the previous 180-degree rotation of the TL block, I've confirmed that the TR block is derived by a 90-degree clockwise rotation of the input grid. This is because the transformation from input coordinates (r, c) to output coordinates for the TR block is (r, c) -> (c, 2-r), which translates directly to a clockwise rotation. I'm now verifying this using the example data.\n\n\n",
